🧠 OOP Concept Demonstrated

The program uses runtime polymorphism through the abstract "Employee" class. The "calculateSalary()" function is overridden by both derived classes, allowing each employee type to calculate salary according to its own rules.

🎯 Objective

To understand how abstract classes and runtime polymorphism can be used to calculate salaries differently for different types of employees.

🔑 Key Concepts Demonstrated

- Abstraction – Hides the implementation details of salary calculation.
- Inheritance – "PermanentEmployee" and "ContractEmployee" inherit from "Employee".
- Polymorphism – Different employee types implement "calculateSalary()" differently.
- Pure Virtual Function – Defines the salary calculation interface.
- Function Overriding – Derived classes provide their own salary calculation.
- Encapsulation – Employee information and operations are grouped within classes.
- Constructors – Used to initialize employee details.
